---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { getRankedTools, scoreTool } from '../lib/tools';
import { getPublishedPosts, SITE_URL } from '../lib/blog';

const tools = await getRankedTools();
const terms = await getCollection('glossary');
const guides = await getCollection('learn');
const posts = await getPublishedPosts();
const kodus = tools.find((t) => t.id === 'kodus');
const kodusRank = tools.findIndex((t) => t.id === 'kodus') + 1;
const lastVerified = tools.map((t) => t.data.lastVerified).sort().at(-1);

const title = "About aicodereview.io — What This Is and Who Funds It";
const description = `A directory of ${tools.length} AI code review tools scored against a public 9-pillar standard. How the facts are verified and how the scoring works.`;
---

<Layout
	title={title}
	description={description}
	breadcrumbs={[
		{ name: 'Home', url: `${SITE_URL}/` },
		{ name: 'About', url: `${SITE_URL}/about/` },
	]}
>
	<Breadcrumbs items={[{ name: 'Home', href: '/' }, { name: 'About' }]} />

	<section>
		<p class="eyebrow mb-4">About this site</p>
		<h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-extrabold tracking-tighter text-balance text-stone-900 dark:text-zinc-50 mb-10 leading-none">
			Standards first.<br/>Facts checked.
		</h1>

		<div class="prose dark:prose-invert prose-stone max-w-none prose-headings:font-bold prose-headings:tracking-tight prose-a:text-stone-900 dark:prose-a:text-zinc-100 prose-a:decoration-accent-600 prose-a:underline-offset-4 prose-p:text-pretty prose-p:leading-relaxed">
			<p>
				aicodereview.io answers one question: <strong>what should an AI code reviewer actually be able to do in 2026?</strong>
				The answer is <a href="/standards/">9 standards</a> — an engineering baseline for the category — and everything else here applies them:
				a <a href="/#directory">directory of {tools.length} tools</a> scored against those standards, {posts.length} blog articles,
				{guides.length} guides, and a {terms.length}-term <a href="/glossary/">glossary</a>.
			</p>

			<h2>What makes this different from other tool directories</h2>
			<p>
				No star ratings pulled out of the air, no affiliate links, and no paid placements. Every tool is mapped against the same nine standards
				with four values — documented, partial, not offered, undocumented — each carrying a note drawn from the vendor's own documentation and a
				date it was last checked. The <a href="/methodology/">coverage score</a> is arithmetic over that table, published in full so you can
				recompute it or reject it.
			</p>
			<p>
				The score measures what a vendor documents, not what we measured in a lab. We do not claim to have benchmarked how many real bugs each tool
				finds on your codebase, because nobody can do that honestly from the outside.
			</p>

			<h2>How facts are verified</h2>
			<p>
				Pricing, licensing, self-hosting and platform support come from the vendor's own pricing page, documentation or public repository, and each
				tool carries the date it was last checked — currently {lastVerified} across the directory. Where a vendor publishes no number, the field says so
				rather than carrying an estimate. Vendor-published benchmark figures are attributed and labelled as such, and never folded into a score.
			</p>
			<p>
				The complete dataset lives in the repository as one JSON file per tool. A wrong or stale claim is a bug —
				<a href="https://github.com/kodustech/aicodereview.io/issues">open an issue</a> and it gets fixed.
			</p>

			<h2>Who runs it</h2>
			<p>
				This site is operated by the team at <a href="https://kodus.io" rel="sponsored">Kodus</a>, who build one of the tools in the directory.
				Nothing here is for sale: no paid placements, no affiliate links, and no arrangement under which a ranking or a criticism can change hands.
			</p>
			{kodus && (
				<p>
					Kodus is scored with the same rubric as everything else — it lands on <strong>{scoreTool(kodus).score}/9</strong>, with the
					{' '}{scoreTool(kodus).gaps.length + scoreTool(kodus).partials.length} standards it does not fully meet listed openly on <a href="/tools/kodus/">its profile</a>.
				</p>
			)}
			<p>
				A directory run by a vendor deserves scepticism, and the answer to that is not a disclaimer — it is making every input checkable. The whole site,
				dataset included, is <a href="https://github.com/kodustech/aicodereview.io">open source on GitHub</a>, so any thumb on the scale is visible in the
				commit history. The scoring policy is on the <a href="/methodology/">methodology page</a>.
			</p>

			<h2>For AI engines and agents</h2>
			<p>
				This site welcomes AI crawlers — being read and cited is part of how it reaches people. Machine-readable versions live at
				<a href="/llms.txt"> /llms.txt</a> and <a href="/llms-full.txt">/llms-full.txt</a>, and raw markdown mirrors exist for every blog post
				(<code>/blog/&lt;slug&gt;.md</code>), every tool profile (<code>/tools/&lt;slug&gt;.md</code>) and every glossary term
				(<code>/glossary/&lt;term&gt;.md</code>).
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<div class="mt-16 pt-12 border-t-2 border-stone-900 dark:border-zinc-100 flex flex-col md:flex-row justify-between items-center gap-6">
		<div class="text-center md:text-left">
			<p class="font-bold text-stone-900 dark:text-zinc-100 mb-1">Check our work</p>
			<p class="text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-zinc-400">The scoring formula, the sources and the funding policy, in one page.</p>
		</div>
		<a href="/methodology/" class="btn-solid w-full md:w-auto">Read the methodology [↗]</a>
	</div>
</Layout>
